Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Bowl Keeps Singing After the Chord
- Story & Context: The opening track establishes the central rule of Mercury Monolith. Eliska begins alone on two closely tuned glass bowls whose beating cycle lasts several seconds. Tomas enters with one enormous baritone-guitar chord, but Nadim cuts the dry guitar after the initial bloom and lets only a filtered tape return remain. Klara waits until the distorted low mids have vanished before entering with a soft contrabass-clarinet fundamental.

Marek strikes one floor tom every two bars and no cymbal for the first half. The glass tones continue through each empty measure, making the listener hear the chord less as a continuous wall and more as a temporary disturbance in an already-existing spectral field.

In the final section, the guitar does not return. Eliska sustains a high beating interval while Klara descends by semitone beneath it. The last audible event is the tape echo of the original guitar chord, now reduced to a dark low-frequency blur.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Close cinematic photograph of wet fingertips touching rotating glass bowls in a tiled laboratory, a dark baritone guitar and contrabass clarinet reflected in the glass, one pressure gauge reading near zero, dim green equipment light. No full lyrics; sparse spoken fragments limited to "after the chord," "leave the bowl turning," "do not fill the room," and "the return is enough."

Track 2: Pressure Gauge at Zero
- Story & Context: The second track is built around total removal of low-frequency pressure. Tomas and Marek begin with the heaviest passage on the EP: slow distorted fifths, one kick per bar, and a bowed cymbal creating a narrow metallic whine. At the end of the fourth cycle, Nadim removes guitar, kick, and low sine simultaneously.

The pressure gauge mounted in the room becomes a visual cue rather than a sound source. While the ensemble remains almost silent, Klara plays a breath-only contrabass-clarinet phrase and Eliska introduces a glass cluster a minor second above the previous guitar root. The absence lasts sixteen bars.

When the guitar returns, it does so without percussion. One low chord enters beneath the glass cluster, creating greater apparent weight than the earlier full-band passage despite containing fewer elements. The track ends with the spring return of a single rim hit repeating into silence.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Old brass pressure gauge fixed to white tile with the needle resting at zero, blurred glass armonica behind it, black amplifier cabinet and contrabass clarinet at the edge of frame, cold laboratory realism. Spoken fragments limited to "zero pressure," "sixteen empty," "one chord only," and "let the spring continue."

Track 3: Condensation Maps the Feedback Loop
- Story & Context: The EP closer begins after the laboratory heating has been shut down. Moisture accumulates on the glass tubing and observation windows while Nadim builds the longest feedback path on the record. Eliska plays single armonica tones into tape delay whose repeats are progressively low-passed until they resemble distant organ pipes.

Tomas contributes only three guitar chords during the entire first half. Klara threads slow chromatic intervals between them, while Marek bows tam-tam and cymbal edges without striking them. The music becomes denser through overlapping decay rather than more notes.

At the midpoint, Nadim closes the feedback loop abruptly. Every return stops. The sudden dry room exposes finger friction on glass, clarinet key noise, and amplifier hiss. For the final twenty-four bars, the band performs with no artificial reverb or delay at all.

The last guitar chord is allowed to decay naturally into the tiled room. Eliska stops the glass bowls one by one, and the final audible sound is the mechanical slowing of the armonica spindle.
